\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{A common lower bound along the angular deformation}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{For the path of \ref{fdip-01C7} put
##{B_0=100(n+|\mu|+1),\quad Q=100B_0,\quad
g_0=\gamma /(4Q),\quad g=g_0/(8Q).}
The \vocabk{frozen symbols}{fdip-01C3} have gain at least #{g}, and their coefficients satisfy both bounds with constant #{Q} in \ref{fdip-01C5}.}

\proof{\p{The signed-hop formula in \ref{fdip-01A5} bounds #{\|H'_\lambda\|} by #{|k-\lambda|+2k}, which is smaller than #{B_0} at the three stated masses. The two pole endpoints stay bounded by #{B_0} under flattening and conjugation. Each coefficient at a pole then has norm at most #{2B_0} by the displayed formula of \ref{fdip-01C7}, including the final scalar damping. Each cutoff is bounded by #{1} and has Lipschitz constant at most #{4}. Thus each of the three Laurent coefficients on the two bands is bounded by #{4B_0} with Lipschitz constant at most #{16B_0}. Initially only the zeroth coefficient varies with #{y}, with Lipschitz constant at most #{1} from the cosine mass shift. Its norm and those of the two constant coefficients are less than #{4B_0}. Affine interpolation preserves these estimates. Adding #{\operatorname{Lip}(d)\le 1} gives the required #{Q}.}

\p{Before the last scalar damping, at either pole the symbol is #{R-\ui\sin x\,\bbI} for Hermitian #{R}. All singular values are at least #{|\sin x|} by squaring. When #{|\sin x|<g_0}, the norm difference from #{R} to the closer endpoint #{A} or #{D} is at most #{B_0(1-|\cos x|)\le B_0|\sin x|\le\gamma /4}. Those endpoints have gain at least #{\gamma}. During the scalar damping, the equal-sign entry has absolute real part #{1}, and the opposite-sign entry has unit modulus. Thus the least singular value of the frozen block at each pole is at least #{g_0}.}

\p{For any frozen angle #{y}, squaring its block eliminates the off-diagonal since #{d(y)} is scalar. Its gain is at least #{|\sin y|}. Within distance #{g_0/(2Q)} of a pole, comparison with that pole costs at most #{Q} times the distance and keeps gain #{g_0/2}. Else, writing #{\eta} for the distance to the nearer pole, one has #{|\sin y|\ge 2\eta /\pi\ge g_0/(\pi Q)}. Both give the stated lower bound.}}
